utting it all out there  so maybe it helps someone and emailed it  to colleagues as a goodbye one to the  forest service i needed real help and  not just talking to friends and drinking  professional help  other letters followed to whom it made  concern i'm resigning after 17 years  with the forest service mike west  detailed his trouble getting treatment  for ptsd the toll on home life and the  pay which even after president biden  intervened last year what i still think  is not enough starts at just 15 an hour  that's far below what local agencies  like cal fire pay if you're putting an  incredible strain on your family and  risking your life you deserve to be able  to support your family without relying  on insane amounts of overtime  west became a teacher humphrey took an  easier higher paying fire job with the  utility company many others left too and  yet the forest service repeatedly told  congress this spring they were on track  to be fully staffed we have brought on  about 90 of that already and we'll get  there 100 percent uh shortly what are  you seeing on the ground that sounded  impossible to scott burkhart a  california hotshot boss who lost 40  percent of his crew this year i respect  their decisions i respect  them going to make more money but it's  hard  the replacements attracted to adventure  and beauty can't always afford housing  you have guys living in their vehicle  affirmative yes  in frustration burkhardt took one of the  resignation letters he had received and  forwarded it to the forest service  leadership along with his own grim  message watching the mass exodus of our  operational knowledge is one of the  saddest evolutions i have witnessed his  resignation embodies every deficiency  that will contribute to the biblical  destruction ahead of us  when we met chief moore early this month  he conceded they had not hired all 11  300 firefighters they were hoping for  currently we're just under 9 000  employees which pushes about 80 percent  